<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bartoszyce</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Bartenstein</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bartoszyce, German Bartenstein, is a town in the Warmian-Masurian Voivodeship of north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyna">Łyna</a> river near the Russian border. It grew around a castle built by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a> around 1240 in the Prussian territory of Barta, which the Prussians besieged during the uprising of the 1260s. It was part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-prussia">East Prussia</a> until 1945.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bartoszyce.jpg" alt="Bartoszyce" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Szymon Siarkowski · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Plac_Konstytucji_3_Maja_w_Bartoszycach.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Bartoszyce lies on the left shore of the Łyna River in a valley, about 90 kilometres east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elblag">Elbląg</a> and 55 kilometres south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konigsberg">Kaliningrad</a>, at an altitude of 3 metres. It is the capital of Bartoszyce County and has 20,767 inhabitants. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Knights</a> built their castle on the border between the Old Prussian regions of Natangia and Bartia, and it belonged to the district of Balga.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-prussians">Old Prussians</a> besieged the castle for four years during an uprising that began in 1260, and it was destroyed in 1264. The Order rebuilt it, and after the uprisings ended it was rebuilt in stone in 1274–80. A settlement developed across the river, first documented in 1326 as Rosenthal; it received town privileges from the Grand Master Luther von <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Braunschweig</a> in 1332 and was renamed Bartenstein. Poles settled there in sizeable numbers from the 14th to the 17th century and used the names Bartoszyce and Barsztyn. In 1440 the town joined the anti-Teutonic Prussian Confederation, and by the Peace of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torun">Toruń</a> of 1466 it became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a> held by the State of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a>.</p><p>The town became Protestant in 1525, when the Order's Prussian lands were secularised as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duchy-of-prussia">Duchy of Prussia</a>, and in 1701 it passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Kingdom of Prussia</a>. Prussia and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> signed the Treaty of Bartenstein there on 26 April 1807. A railway reached it in 1868 and brought an iron foundry, a machine factory and a train-car factory. It became the district capital in 1902, and the district was renamed Landkreis Bartenstein in 1927. About half the town was destroyed in fighting between German and Soviet forces in 1945, and on 15 June 1945 the Soviets handed it to Polish authorities. The remaining German residents were evacuated or expelled and the town was repopulated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Bartoszyce belongs to the Warmian-Masurian Voivodeship, which it joined in 1999 after lying in Olsztyn Voivodeship from 1946. It is a member of Cittaslow. The future priest Jerzy Popiełuszko did his military service in a special unit there in 1966–1968, and a memorial to him stands in the town. A scene of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leo-tolstoy">Leo Tolstoy</a>'s War and Peace is set there.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bartoszyce” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Bartians inhabited Barta, an inland Prussian territory around modern <b>Bartoszyce</b> in north-eastern Poland, divided into Greater and Lesser Barta.</blockquote><a href="/state/bartians">Bartians</a></li></ul></section>
